**Design: Intrusive Free List Pool**

At initialization, allocate a large contiguous block of memory and partition it into fixed-size chunks. Maintain a singly-linked free list through the chunks themselves (store the `next` pointer in the first bytes of each free chunk).

```cpp
class MemoryPool {
private:
    struct FreeBlock {
        FreeBlock* next;
    };

    void* pool_;           // base pointer
    FreeBlock* free_head_; // head of free list
    size_t block_size_;
    size_t num_blocks_;

public:
    MemoryPool(size_t block_size, size_t num_blocks)
        : block_size_(std::max(block_size, sizeof(FreeBlock*))),
          num_blocks_(num_blocks) {
        // Single allocation at startup
        pool_ = ::malloc(block_size_ * num_blocks_);

        // Initialize free list
        free_head_ = static_cast<FreeBlock*>(pool_);
        FreeBlock* current = free_head_;
        for (size_t i = 1; i < num_blocks_; ++i) {
            FreeBlock* next = reinterpret_cast<FreeBlock*>(
                static_cast<char*>(pool_) + i * block_size_);
            current->next = next;
            current = next;
        }
        current->next = nullptr;
    }

    void* alloc() {   // O(1)
        if (!free_head_) return nullptr;
        void* block = free_head_;
        free_head_ = free_head_->next;
        return block;
    }

    void free(void* ptr) {  // O(1)
        FreeBlock* block = static_cast<FreeBlock*>(ptr);
        block->next = free_head_;
        free_head_ = block;
    }
};
```

**Complexity analysis:**
- `alloc()`: $O(1)$ -- pop from free list head
- `free()`: $O(1)$ -- push to free list head
- Memory overhead: zero (the free list pointer is stored in the unused block)
- Cache behavior: sequential allocation from the pool is cache-friendly

**Why this matters for trading:**
- System `malloc` may take $1{-}100\mu s$ due to lock contention and page faults
- Pool allocator takes $< 10ns$ (a few pointer operations)
- No fragmentation since all blocks are the same size
- Deterministic latency (no garbage collection, no compaction)
